    <body>
      <sec id="article-27930.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pancreatic pseudocysts are well-circumscribed, encapsulated collections of enzyme-rich fluid that most commonly develop after acute pancreatitis, chronic pancreatitis, or pancreatic trauma. Although many pseudocysts resolve with conservative care, accurate diagnosis remains clinically important because these lesions must be distinguished from walled-off pancreatic necrosis, cystic neoplasms, and other causes of abdominal symptoms. A persistent practice gap exists in the timely recognition, evaluation, and management of pancreatic pseudocysts, particularly in differentiating uncomplicated lesions from those requiring intervention for infection, hemorrhage, obstruction, or rupture. This activity addresses that gap by reviewing the pathogenesis, clinical presentation, imaging evaluation, differential diagnosis, conservative monitoring, and indications for endoscopic, percutaneous, or surgical treatment. Participants are expected to gain improved diagnostic accuracy, evidence-based management strategies, better risk assessment for complications, and stronger interdisciplinary coordination to support safe, patient-centered care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate pancreatic pseudocysts from walled-off pancreatic necrosis, pancreatic cystic neoplasms, and other conditions with similar clinical or imaging features.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine which patients are appropriate for conservative management and monitoring versus invasive intervention.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select evidence-based endoscopic, percutaneous, or surgical treatment strategies based on symptoms, complications, ductal anatomy, and procedural feasibility.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with gastroenterologists, radiologists, surgeons, and other members of the interprofessional healthcare team to coordinate safe, patient-centered care for patients with pancreatic pseudocysts.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27930&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27930">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>A pancreatic pseudocyst is a well-circumscribed, encapsulated collection of enzyme-rich fluid surrounded by a nonepithelialized wall composed of inflammatory tissue with little if any necrosis, usually maturing more than 4 weeks after the onset of pancreatitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r1">[1]</xref> These collections are usually located in the peripancreatic region, commonly in the lesser sac, and most commonly arise as a complication of acute pancreatitis, particularly in moderate-to-severe cases. Pseudocysts may develop more insidiously in the setting of chronic pancreatitis, in which cases they are often associated with ductal obstruction or fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r3">[3]</xref> Pseudocysts also may occur as a consequence of pancreatic trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Pseudocysts form due to the disruption of the pancreatic ductal system, leading to the leakage of enzyme-rich pancreatic fluid. Alcohol use and gallstone disease are the most common underlying causes. Clinical presentation is typically nonspecific, with abdominal pain, early satiety, and nausea or vomiting being the most common symptoms. Although imaging features such as a well-defined, thick-walled, homogenous fluid collection in the setting of recent pancreatitis strongly suggest a pseudocyst, careful differentiation from cystic neoplasms is important, particularly in patients older than 40 years or in the absence of a clear history of pancreatitis. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CT) remains the first-line imaging modality (see <bold>Image.</bold> Pancreatic Pseudocyst).&#x000a0;However, MRI with magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography and endoscopic ultrasonography are increasingly used to assess cyst content, ductal anatomy, and features suggestive of neoplasia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Most pseudocysts resolve spontaneously, especially if asymptomatic, and size alone is not an indication for intervention. Current guidelines recommend conservative management for asymptomatic pseudocysts, regardless of size. Intervention is reserved for those with symptoms or complications such as infection, hemorrhage, or obstruction of the stomach, duodenum, or bile duct.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;This activity reviews the etiology, pathogenesis, clinical features, evaluation, and management of pancreatic pseudocysts, as well as the role of interdisciplinary teams in their treatment.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Pseudocysts most commonly arise as complications of acute or chronic pancreatitis, with the underlying mechanism involving disruption of the pancreatic duct and extravasation of enzyme-rich fluid into surrounding tissue, which subsequently becomes encapsulated by granulation and fibrous tissue. This process may also occur following pancreatic trauma or surgical procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref> After the onset of acute pancreatitis, pseudocyst formation generally occurs within 4 to 6 weeks as a fluid collection matures and develops a defined wall, and must be distinguished from other acute inflammatory pancreatic fluid collections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r9">[9]</xref> In chronic pancreatitis, recurrent ductal obstruction from fibrosis, protein plugs, or calcifications may promote pseudocyst development, often during disease exacerbations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>The most common etiology of pseudocysts in adults living in countries with high alcohol consumption is alcoholic pancreatitis, accounting for approximately 60% to 80% of all pseudocysts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r10">[10]</xref> However, alcohol use itself does not increase the likelihood of developing pseudocysts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref> Idiopathic causes represent the next most common etiology, followed by gallstone disease, blunt or penetrating trauma, and surgical procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r11">[11]</xref> Pseudocysts can occur&#x000a0;in any portion of the pancreas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Pancreatic pseudocysts occur in about 1 in 100,000 adults annually. Pseudocysts can develop as a complication of pancreatitis at any age but are more common in adults, with a slight male predominance reflecting the epidemiology of pancreatitis itself. The incidence of pseudocysts as a consequence of any cause of pancreatitis is 1% to 4.5%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r12">[12]</xref> Most pseudocysts arise following acute pancreatitis, with an incidence of 5% to 16%. Pseudocysts are a more frequent complication of chronic pancreatitis (occurring in 10% to 30% of cases), but their prevalence is lower than after acute pancreatitis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Injury to the pancreatic ductal system, either from pancreatitis or direct trauma, can lead to extravasation of pancreatic fluid into the retroperitoneum, causing autolysis of surrounding tissue. In some instances, this inflammatory process stimulates the development of a fluid collection with a fibrous nonepithelial capsule, confined by walls of adjacent organs such as the stomach, pancreas, omentum, and colon, forming a pseudocyst. The lack of epithelium distinguishes a pseudocyst from a true cyst or cystic neoplasm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r3">[3]</xref> The pseudocyst wall generally takes 4 to 6 weeks to mature.</p>
        <p>Pseudocysts developing as a complication of chronic pancreatitis can result from pancreatic ductal stones, proteinaceous plugs, strictures, or an acute exacerbation of pancreatitis. Pancreatic juice may extravasate in this setting when increased ductal pressure causes rupture of the main pancreatic duct or a side branch.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r3">[3]</xref> Up to two-thirds of pseudocysts have demonstrable connections to the pancreatic duct. In the remaining one-third of cases, an inflammatory reaction can sometimes seal off the connection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>In the appropriate clinical context, such as a well-defined cystic mass near the pancreas following recent pancreatitis, imaging findings can strongly suggest a pancreatic pseudocyst. However, differentiation from neoplastic cystic lesions, especially mucinous cystadenomas, remains crucial, particularly in patients without a clear history of pancreatitis or those older than 40 years. The American College of Gastroenterology recommends endoscopic ultrasound-guided&#x000a0;fine needle aspiration (EUS-FNA) for cyst fluid analysis when the diagnosis is uncertain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Cytology obtained via EUS-FNA is limited by low cellularity and sampling variability. Histologic samples from cyst wall biopsies may include benign pseudocyst tissue, areas of dysplasia, or malignant cells, depending on the biopsy site, so a negative cytology result does not reliably exclude a neoplastic diagnosis. Therefore, cyst fluid analysis, including carcinoembryonic antigen levels and cytology, should be interpreted in conjunction with imaging findings, clinical history, and, when available, molecular markers to optimize diagnostic accuracy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>No pathognomonic symptoms are specific to a pancreatic pseudocyst. However, in patients with a recent history of acute or chronic pancreatitis, the development of new symptoms should raise clinical suspicion for a pseudocyst. The American College of Gastroenterology notes that most pseudocysts arise in the setting of known pancreatitis and that symptoms are often due to mass effect or complications such as infection, hemorrhage, or gastrointestinal tract or biliary obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Physical examination may often be unremarkable unless the pseudocyst is large or complicated. Therefore, clinical suspicion should be guided by the overall clinical context, including a history of pancreatitis, symptom duration, and patient age. Common clinical features that may suggest the presence of a pseudocyst include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent abdominal pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anorexia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A new palpable abdominal mass following pancreatitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Jaundice or signs of shock (usually in complicated cases)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Additional findings&#x000a0;that have limited sensitivity and specificity include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Fever</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scleral icterus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Abdominal tenderness</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Signs of peritonitis (eg, guarding or rigidity), particularly in the setting of cyst rupture</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A left-sided pleural effusion&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r5">[5]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The evaluation of pancreatic pseudocysts begins with clinical suspicion, particularly in patients with a history of acute or chronic pancreatitis, as serum enzyme levels (amylase and lipase) may be normal or elevated and offer limited diagnostic utility.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Transabdominal ultrasonography</bold> is often the initial imaging modality due to its wide availability, safety, and cost-effectiveness. Transabdominal ultrasonography has a sensitivity of 70% to 90% for detecting pancreatic fluid collections, but is highly operator-dependent, and its accuracy may vary with the patient's body habitus.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Contrast-enhanced CT</bold> is the imaging modality of choice for initial evaluation. Contrast-enhanced CT has been reported to have a sensitivity of 82% to 100% and a specificity of up to 98% for identifying pancreatic fluid collections. CT is preferred due to its ability to delineate cyst size, location, wall thickness, and the presence of necrotic debris or calcifications, and to distinguish pseudocysts from walled-off necrosis. However, CT cannot reliably differentiate pseudocysts from cystic neoplasms, which may have a similar radiographic appearance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>EUS</bold>&#x000a0;offers superior spatial resolution and is particularly valuable when neoplastic cysts are suspected or when cross-sectional imaging is inconclusive. EUS allows for real-time imaging of the cyst wall and contents (usually amylase-rich fluid without necrotic tissue or other solid debris). EUS also facilitates FNA for cytology and fluid analysis, as well as therapeutic drainage. EUS features that raise suspicion for neoplasia rather than a pseudocyst include cyst wall thickness greater than 3 mm, septations, mural nodules, and dilation of the pancreatic duct.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cyst fluid analysis:</bold>&#x000a0;Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) is the most commonly used biomarker to distinguish mucinous from nonmucinous cysts.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Pancreatic pseudocysts are nonmucinous cystic lesions and typically have low CEA levels and high amylase or lipase levels. Although a cyst fluid CEA level greater than 192 ng/mL is widely cited as a diagnostic cutoff for mucinous differentiation (for mucinous cystadenomas and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms [IPMN]),<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r15">[15]</xref> dysplasia in IPMNs cannot be predicted based on the degree of CEA elevation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>CEA also does not indicate a neoplastic diagnosis&#x000a0;in mucinous cysts; the marker reflects only mucinous differentiation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r5">[5]</xref> CEA levels greater than 400 ng/mL demonstrated 57% sensitivity and 100% specificity in ruling out pseudocysts and identifying mucinous cystic lesions and cystadenocarcinomas, and CEA levels less than 4 ng/mL demonstrated 100% sensitivity and 93% specificity in distinguishing serous cystadenomas from pseudocysts, mucinous cystic neoplasms, and cancer.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r16">[16]</xref> Other useful studies of pancreatic cyst fluid include cytology and biomarker testing for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">KRAS</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">TP53</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic>, and others.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r17">[17]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>MRI with magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography </bold>(MRCP) provides excellent contrast resolution and detailed evaluation of pancreatic ductal anatomy. MRI with MRCP is especially useful when CT findings are inconclusive or when characterizing complex fluid content within the pseudocyst (eg, debris versus necrosis). MRI with MRCP may also be used for preprocedural planning to assess ductal communication, though this is usually unnecessary if CT provides sufficient detail.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Overall, the diagnosis of a pancreatic pseudocyst relies on integrating clinical context with imaging findings. In cases where a neoplastic or premalignant cyst is suspected, multimodal evaluation with EUS and MRI with MRCP may be warranted to guide further management.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Spontaneous resolution of pancreatic pseudocysts is common, particularly those arising after an episode of acute pancreatitis. For uncomplicated, asymptomatic, or stable pseudocysts, the mainstay of management is conservative care, including supportive measures such as analgesics, antiemetics, and dietary modifications (typically a low-fat diet). Patients should be monitored with interval imaging to assess for changes in size or the development of complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Spontaneous resolution is less common in pseudocysts associated with chronic pancreatitis, but still occurs, especially in single lesions without associated ductal abnormalities or complications. Factors associated with a lower likelihood of resolution include multiple cysts, tail location, pancreatic duct strictures or calcifications, and progressive growth. However, size alone is not an absolute indication of drainage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Urgent intervention is warranted in cases complicated by infection, hemorrhage, gastric outlet or biliary obstruction, or peritonitis. Alarming symptoms that should prompt evaluation for complications include worsening abdominal pain, persistent nausea or vomiting, inability to tolerate oral intake, fever, chills, or hypotension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;The 3 main categories of invasive management are:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Endoscopic drainage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Percutaneous drainage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical drainage or resection</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Among these, endoscopic drainage is now the preferred approach for symptomatic or complicated pseudocysts, as recommended by the American Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy and supported by recent meta-analyses. In most cases, endoscopic drainage offers lower morbidity and recurrence rates than percutaneous or surgical options. Percutaneous drainage is generally reserved for patients who are poor surgical candidates or for pseudocysts that are inaccessible by endoscopy, as it is associated with higher rates of recurrence and fistula formation.</p>
        <p>Surgical drainage or resection is typically considered when endoscopic approaches fail or are not feasible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;When feasible, intervention should be delayed for at least 6 weeks after the initial pancreatitis episode to allow for maturation and encapsulation of the pseudocyst wall, improving the safety and success of drainage. However, urgent intervention should not be delayed if life-threatening complications are suspected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Endoscopic Drainage</bold>
</p>
        <p>Endoscopic drainage is now the first-line and most widely used treatment for symptomatic or complicated pancreatic pseudocysts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r19">[19]</xref> Endoscopic drainage offers a minimally invasive approach with high long-term success and avoids the need for external drainage. The primary goal is to establish internal drainage into the gastrointestinal tract using either transmural drainage (TSM) or transpapillary drainage (TPD).</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Transmural drainage:&#x000a0;</bold>TSM involves creating a fistulous tract between the pseudocyst and the gastric or duodenal lumen when the cyst wall is in direct apposition to the gastrointestinal tract.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Using EUS guidance, a needle is used to access the pseudocyst, followed by dilation and stent deployment to allow drainage. Both double-pigtail plastic stents and lumen-apposing metal stents draining the pseudocysts into the stomach or, less often, the duodenum are considered appropriate for uncomplicated cases, and no clear superiority of either over the other has been established.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r20">[20]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>TSM is the preferred approach when the pseudocyst is adherent to the gastrointestinal wall and does not require ductal communication. EUS-guided drainage has higher technical success rates (approximately 95%) than non&#x02013;EUS-guided approaches (approximately 59%) and reduces complications, such as bleeding, by identifying intervening vessels. Major adverse effects include hemorrhage, perforation, and infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r21">[21]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Transpapillary drainage:</bold> TPD is used when the pseudocyst communicates with the main pancreatic duct or is not in direct contact with the gastrointestinal lumen. During endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography, a catheter is passed into the pancreatic duct, and a stent is deployed into the tract between the duct and the pseudocyst to facilitate drainage. Balloon dilation may be required if ductal strictures are present. TPD is less effective for large or multiple pseudocysts or those located in or near the tail of the pancreas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r22">[22]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Percutaneous Drainage&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Percutaneous drainage is typically performed under ultrasonography or CT guidance, with placement of a pigtail catheter into the pseudocyst cavity. The catheter remains in place until fluid output decreases significantly. Although technically effective, percutaneous drainage often causes patient discomfort due to catheter clogging and the need for frequent repositioning. Percutaneous drainage&#x000a0;also requires ongoing catheter care, making it less suitable for patients unable to manage an external drain at home; this is contraindicated in the presence of pancreatic ductal strictures or disconnected duct syndrome, as persistent leakage may lead to external pancreatic fistulas.</p>
        <p>This procedure is generally reserved for specific clinical scenarios, such as critically ill individuals who cannot tolerate endoscopic or surgical intervention, infected or immature pseudocysts that are not endoscopically accessible, or cases in which wall maturation is insufficient for transmural drainage. Results&#x000a0;from recent studies and meta-analyses indicate that&#x000a0;percutaneous drainage is inferior to endoscopic drainage in terms of clinical success, recurrence rate, reintervention rate, and hospital length of stay. Compared to surgical drainage,&#x000a0;percutaneous drainage is associated with higher complication rates, including mortality, longer hospitalization, and need for reintervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Treatment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Historically, surgical drainage into the stomach or small bowel was the standard method for treating pancreatic pseudocysts. However, with the evolution of endoscopic techniques, surgical procedures are now typically reserved for select settings, such as biliary or gastric outlet obstruction, recurrent or persistent pseudocysts, a strictured or dilated main pancreatic duct, cysts of uncertain origin, a neoplastic diagnosis, collections not amenable to endoscopic access, or failure of endoscopic or percutaneous drainage. Currently, surgical approaches include internal drainage, pancreatic resection, and external drainage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Drainage is also performed when a wider stoma is required, when a cholecystectomy is required (in the context of biliary pancreatitis), or when debridement is necessary.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Internal drainage</bold> (cystogastrostomy, cystoduodenostomy, or cystojejunostomy) requires a mature capsule, so this approach must be performed at least 4 to 6 weeks after the onset of pancreatitis. If a surgical procedure must be performed earlier in the course of pancreatitis, pancreatic resection or external drainage is performed instead. Partial pancreatectomy is performed when there are multiple or distal pancreatic pseudocysts, pseudoaneurysms, associated main pancreatic ductal dilatation, or if cancer cannot be excluded.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Many of these operations can be performed <bold>laparoscopically</bold>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r21">[21]</xref> Laparoscopic drainage is favored over laparotomy when feasible due to lower morbidity, shorter hospital stays, and faster recovery. Results from a systematic review reported a 98.3% technical success rate, a 2.5% recurrence rate, a mean hospital stay of 5.7 days, and a complication rate of less than 2% for laparoscopic drainage.</p>
        <p>Although results from retrospective studies suggest that surgical drainage may have a slightly higher success rate in complex or refractory cases, endoscopic drainage remains the first-line approach, as endorsed by the American Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy and supported by cost-effectiveness analyses. Surgical drainage remains a safe and effective alternative when wider stoma formation is required, a cholecystectomy is required (in the context of biliary pancreatitis), necrotic tissue must be debrided, the etiology is unclear, or less invasive approaches have failed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of pancreatic pseudocysts includes the following:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Walled-off p</bold><bold>ancreatic n</bold><bold>ecrosis</bold>: This is a late complication of acute necrotizing pancreatitis characterized by a mature, encapsulated collection containing both fluid and solid necrotic debris. Imaging shows heterogeneous collections with nonliquid components, distinguishing them from pseudocysts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pancreatic artery pseudoaneurysm</bold>: This is a complication of pancreatitis caused by arterial erosion, leading to rapid cyst enlargement, a sudden decrease in hemoglobin levels, and upper gastrointestinal bleeding.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pancreatic cystic neoplasms</bold>: These include mucinous cystic neoplasms, intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms, serous cystadenomas, solid pseudopapillary tumors, pancreatic cystadenocarcinomas (usually arising from progression of mucinous cystic neoplasms), and cystic pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors. These lesions appear as multiloculated cysts with thick walls and elevated CEA levels in the cystic fluid. Clinical features include loss of appetite and weight loss.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Other diagnoses</bold> with a similar clinical presentation to a pseudocyst include extrapancreatic pathologies such as peptic ulcer disease, gastric cancer, cholelithiasis, acute cholecystitis, bowel obstruction, intestinal ischemia, ovarian cysts and malignant neoplasms, unstable angina, acute myocardial infarction, or pneumonia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Spontaneous resolution of pancreatic pseudocysts is common, particularly following acute pancreatitis. Most pseudocysts decrease in size or resolve entirely with conservative management, especially when they are asymptomatic and uncomplicated. Recent studies' results report spontaneous resolution rates of 77% to 84%. Pseudocyst maturation, defined as the formation of a well-encapsulated wall, typically occurs over 4 to 6 weeks, though timing may vary by&#x000a0;individual patient factors. According to the American Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy, most acute pancreatic fluid collections do not require intervention unless they become symptomatic or complicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r28">[28]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>While many pseudocysts resolve without intervention, complications may arise and warrant prompt recognition and management. These include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Compressive gastric outlet obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Biliary obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pseudoaneurysm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Erosive bleeding into the pseudocyst (which may simulate a splenic artery aneurysm)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Perforation and rupture, with fistulization into the gastrointestinal tract</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Free rupture into the peritoneal cavity or retroperitoneal space, causing abdominal pain, peritonitis, or even death</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased intra-abdominal pressure from large pseudocysts, presenting with dyspnea, abdominal pain, distention, and organ failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Secondary&#x000a0;portal hypertension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency as a result of an obstructed pancreatic duct or other pancreatic damage&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27930.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Pancreatic pseudocysts typically form after an episode of acute pancreatitis (most often associated with alcohol use and gallstone disease), chronic pancreatitis, or abdominal trauma. While many pseudocysts remain asymptomatic, patients may present with vague and nonspecific symptoms such as persistent abdominal pain, early satiety, nausea, or vomiting. Clinical vigilance is essential, especially in patients with a known history of pancreatitis, because complications such as infection, hemorrhage, or obstruction may develop insidiously.</p>
        <p>Education should focus on recognizing warning signs that require prompt evaluation, including worsening abdominal pain, intractable nausea or vomiting, inability to tolerate oral intake, hematemesis, jaundice, fever, or unintentional weight loss. Patients with a history of pancreatitis should be counseled on lifestyle modifications to reduce recurrence and subsequent pseudocyst formation, including limiting alcohol consumption, managing gallstone disease, and controlling metabolic risk factors such as hypertriglyceridemia and hypercalcemia.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Preventive strategies aimed at reducing the&#x000a0;risk of recurrent pancreatitis can also mitigate the risk of pseudocyst formation. These include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Abstinence from alcohol in at-risk individuals</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Timely treatment of gallstone disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Management of metabolic triggers (eg, hypertriglyceridemia, hypercalcemia)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoidance of abdominal trauma</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Patient education should highlight the importance of follow-up care after pancreatitis, especially if symptoms persist beyond the acute phase.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Management of pancreatic pseudocysts benefits from a coordinated, multidisciplinary approach. Endoscopic drainage is the first-line treatment for symptomatic or complicated pseudocysts, with percutaneous drainage or surgery being required in certain specific settings. Involvement of gastroenterologists, radiologists, and surgeons ensures appropriate patient selection and optimizes outcomes, particularly in complex or refractory cases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27930.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
